# Talos Kubernetes operations

Work from `~/repos/talos-proxmox` (or the sibling checkout) in `devenv shell`. Read its local instructions and current configuration before acting. Use the environment named in the request or established in the session. If neither identifies a target, use dev for initial read-only investigation and clarify the target before mutation. Existing authorization carries forward; do not require another confirmation for each command in an authorized rollout.

Always select the environment's kubeconfig explicitly. Each cluster has its own Argo CD; there is no separate management cluster and neither cluster manages the other.

## Environments and sources of truth

| | Dev | Prod |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Infrastructure | Proxmox `pve`, `pve2`, `pve3` | AWS `us-east-1`, one AZ (`us-east-1a`) |
| Control plane + workloads | `dev-server1`, `10.69.12.11`, 8 CPU / 32 GiB, GPU passthrough | One On-Demand `m6a.xlarge`, 4 vCPU / 16 GiB |
| Baseline workers | `dev-server2` `.12`, 8 CPU / 16 GiB; `dev-server3` `.13`, 4 CPU / 16 GiB | One On-Demand `m6a.xlarge`, 4 vCPU / 16 GiB |
| Elastic capacity | Fixed local VMs; no AWS workers | Karpenter Spot CPU burst pool; optional GPU pool disabled |
| Kubernetes API | Control-plane node directly, port 6443 | Stable control-plane Elastic IP, port 6443 |
| Default PVC storage | Node-local `local-path` | Encrypted gp3 EBS through EBS CSI; single-AZ |
| Object storage | Existing external MinIO, bucket `platform-dev` | Private versioned S3, `platform-prod-<account>-us-east-1` |
| Local kubeconfig | `$HOME/.kube/talos-dev.yaml` | `$HOME/.kube/talos-prod.yaml` |
| Local Talos config | `$HOME/.talos/config-dev.yaml` | `$HOME/.talos/config-prod.yaml` |

The prod baseline is 8 vCPU / 32 GiB before system reservations. Both environments have one control plane; they are not HA. Local-path volumes are node-bound; EBS can reattach to a replacement node in the same AZ but does not make the cluster or a single-instance database HA. MinIO and S3 hold objects, not ordinary PVCs.

Treat this table as orientation, not an inventory cache. Check:

- `terraform/cluster/env/dev/k8s_nodes.json` and `main.tfvars` for Proxmox nodes, addresses and allocations.
- `terraform/cluster/env/prod/main.tfvars` and `terraform/aws/` for AWS networking, instance type and infrastructure ownership. Discover the current Elastic IP from generated access files or AWS; do not hard-code it.
- `apps/components/karpenter-nodes/values.yaml` and `environments/prod/values.yaml` for current worker types, replica targets and bounds.
- `apps/argocd/platform/values.yaml` and component overlays for actual deployment membership, namespaces and environment differences.

## Access

Doppler project `talos-proxmox`, configs `dev` and `prod`, holds YAML-valued `KUBECONFIG` and `TALOSCONFIG` secrets. Refresh access files after rebuilds or credential rotation. A destroyed dev environment may have no access secrets until Manual Provision recreates it.

Fetch only the selected environment. Write downloads to private temporary files and move them into place only after successful retrieval, so a failed fetch cannot empty a working config:

```bash
set -e
export CLUSTER_ENV=dev
umask 077
mkdir -p "$HOME/.kube" "$HOME/.talos"
kube_tmp=$(mktemp "$HOME/.kube/.talos-${CLUSTER_ENV}.XXXXXX")
talos_tmp=$(mktemp "$HOME/.talos/.talos-${CLUSTER_ENV}.XXXXXX")
trap 'rm -f "$kube_tmp" "$talos_tmp"' EXIT
doppler secrets get KUBECONFIG --plain --project talos-proxmox --config "$CLUSTER_ENV" > "$kube_tmp"
doppler secrets get TALOSCONFIG --plain --project talos-proxmox --config "$CLUSTER_ENV" > "$talos_tmp"
mv "$kube_tmp" "$HOME/.kube/talos-${CLUSTER_ENV}.yaml"
mv "$talos_tmp" "$HOME/.talos/config-${CLUSTER_ENV}.yaml"
export KUBECONFIG="$HOME/.kube/talos-${CLUSTER_ENV}.yaml"
export TALOSCONFIG="$HOME/.talos/config-${CLUSTER_ENV}.yaml"
kubectl config current-context
kubectl get nodes -o wide
```

The Doppler secret is YAML, not a path: `doppler run -- kubectl` does not select a usable kubeconfig. Do not print credentials, raw Secret objects, complete Helm values or unfiltered OpenTofu plans/state; Helm metadata can expose machine configuration even when inputs were marked sensitive. Keep access files, saved plans and etcd snapshots private and out of Git.

Dev management needs LAN/tailnet routing; CI joins Tailscale. Prod management ports are restricted to declared operator/CI CIDRs. For Talos, use the configured management endpoint and explicitly target the node's private IP; the AWS endpoint is the Elastic IP while the node target is private. Select exactly one control-plane node for an etcd snapshot. Neither cluster uses a Kubernetes API VIP, Cilium LAN pools or L2 announcements.

## Ownership and deployment

- **Foundation**, applied by an operator, owns account identities, generic S3 storage/image assets, Doppler configuration/tokens, GitHub Environments and HCP workspaces.
- **Cluster roots**, `terraform/cluster/dev` and `prod`, own Proxmox/AWS infrastructure, Talos identity/bootstrap and generated access/worker credentials written to Doppler. AWS owns a fixed control-plane EC2 instance; Karpenter owns worker instances.
- **Platform root**, `terraform/platform`, owns bootstrap networking (Gateway API CRDs, Cilium, Talos CCM), ESO authentication, Argo CD, and prod Karpenter/NodePools/EC2NodeClasses. Read the actual resource definitions when an ownership boundary is unclear.
- **Argo CD** in each cluster reconciles its pinned platform revision and component Applications. ESO reads environment-scoped Doppler secrets into Kubernetes. Change the owning Git/Tofu/Doppler configuration rather than leaving a live patch that its controller will revert.

HCP stores and locks state; OpenTofu executes locally or in CI. Select `TF_WORKSPACE=talos-cluster-<env>` or `talos-platform-<env>` inside the devenv command, since entering the shell unsets it. Foundation uses `talos-proxmox`. Export the Doppler `HCP_TERRAFORM_TOKEN` as `TF_TOKEN_app_terraform_io` without displaying it. Roots exchange credentials through Doppler, not remote-state reads.

Trusted repository PRs deploy dev; main deploys dev then prod. Fork PRs validate only. Manual production promotion requires a full main SHA with a successful dev deployment. A successful OpenTofu apply does not prove child Applications or workloads have converged.

Argo CD uses ClusterIP in both environments; access it by management port-forward or its Git-owned Cloudflare tunnel binding. Dev hostnames use `<service>-dev.phuchoang.sbs`; prod uses `<service>.phuchoang.sbs`. Do not replace operator-managed tunnel routes in the Cloudflare dashboard.

## Karpenter and maintenance

Prod's static `aws-workers` pool maintains one On-Demand `m6a.xlarge`; `limits.nodes: 2` allows one replacement overlap. Dynamic `aws-cpu-burst` uses Spot, currently capped at 4 vCPU / 16 GiB (one `m6a.xlarge`), and consolidates after five minutes. Both share `aws-cpu` and one Karpenter controller on the fixed control plane. There is no ASG, Cluster Autoscaler, stateless-only restriction or separate CPU/GPU provisioner. Ordinary PVC workloads may use eligible workers. The optional GPU pool is disabled until explicitly configured.

Karpenter reacts to unschedulable pods and their requests, not low/high node usage; application replica scaling is separate. Compare requests, measured usage and allocatable capacity before shrinking nodes. Account for Talos/system reservations (500m CPU and 1536 MiB per node), DaemonSets, placement constraints and same-AZ EBS attachment. Preserve cloud-provider/Cilium startup taints until their controllers initialize the node. KubePrism is Talos's local API proxy, not an external VIP; AWS does not connect to dev through KubeSpan.

For a control-plane resize, inspect the OpenTofu plan for stop/start versus replacement, preserve the root disk/etcd identity and stable endpoint, and take an etcd snapshot before disruptive maintenance. Single-control-plane API downtime is expected. Recover API, kubelet and Cilium readiness before reducing worker capacity. EC2 resizing may leave the Kubernetes instance-type label stale; compare it with AWS and actual reported capacity. Respect PDBs during drain; inspect blocked pods and their owners before choosing recovery. Do not routinely bypass budgets or remove finalizers.

## Diagnose and verify

Start with selected-environment `get`, `describe`, events and bounded logs. Distinguish unscheduled Pending pods from scheduled pods waiting for images, CNI, admission webhooks or volumes.

```bash
kubectl get --raw=/readyz
kubectl get nodes -o wide
kubectl top nodes
kubectl get applications -n argo-cd
kubectl get pods -A
kubectl get nodepool,ec2nodeclass
kubectl get nodeclaims -o wide
kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/karpenter --tail=100
```

Karpenter resources exist only in prod. For scheduling, inspect pod requests/selectors, NodeClaims, pool limits and disruption events; for storage, inspect PVC/PV topology and CSI attachments; for webhooks, inspect Service endpoints, certificates, controller readiness and network policies before disabling admission.

Use `data` for shared data services and downstream Spark/Flink jobs, `ml` for Kubeflow/KServe/training, and `operators` for shared operators, storage provisioners and Argo Workflows. Retain upstream `knative-serving`, `knative-eventing`, `istio-system`, `auth`, `argo-cd` and `observability` namespaces. Avoid reintroducing application-specific namespaces into platform ownership. Check current manifests when old namespaces are still present during a migration.

Read `docs/operations/cluster-access.md` for access/convergence, `docs/operations/aws-burst-workers.md` for scaling, `docs/architecture/terraform-ci.md` for state/CI, `docs/architecture/gitops.md` for reconciliation, and `docs/reference/secrets.md` for credential ownership. Report the verified nodes, pool bounds, workload health and any unresolved failures separately; do not equate a Ready node with a healthy full platform.
